    summary: "The clinical scenario is specific and common: a treated colorectal cancer patient's serum CEA rises during surveillance while CT, MRI and colonoscopy are normal or equivocal. Salvage surgery or ablation can still cure oligometastatic recurrence, but only if the disease can be localised. Arcitumomab (CEA-Scan), a technetium-99m anti-CEA antibody fragment approved for precisely this indication, was withdrawn from both the US and EU markets in 2005; clinicians now fall back on FDG PET/CT off-label, which in one referred series found recurrence in 56 of 88 such patients.\n\nMeanwhile the therapeutic side of CEACAM5 has grown: antibody-drug conjugates, T-cell engagers and CAR-T programmes target it, and the field has learned that CEA-directed therapy selection is not the same question as CEA-directed localisation. The most advanced CEACAM5-directed imaging agent in humans is not a PET tracer at all but SGM-101, a near-infrared fluorescent anti-CEA antibody for intraoperative use, now in a randomised phase 3 trial (NCT03659448, about 300 patients). For PET, the most advanced is the CEA-targeted nanobody 68Ga-HNI01 at first-in-human scale, nine patients.\n\nThe competitive picture changed independently of imaging. Tumour-informed ctDNA now detects recurrence a median of 5.9 months before radiological recurrence in GALAXY/CIRCULATE-Japan, and ctDNA-guided adjuvant therapy was non-inferior to standard management in DYNAMIC while halving chemotherapy use. ctDNA says whether and roughly when; it does not say where. That distinction is the whole of the case for a CEACAM5 tracer, and it is a stronger case now than when CEA-Scan was withdrawn, because more patients are being flagged as molecularly positive with nothing to localise.",
