    name: "CXCR4 imaging instead of adrenal vein sampling",
    aka: ["PentixaFor PET", "CXCR4 PET in primary aldosteronism"],
    lens: "unmet-need-first",
    tldr: "A common and curable cause of high blood pressure is almost never cured, because finding out which adrenal gland is at fault needs an invasive procedure few hospitals do well. A PET scan can answer the same question, and one is now heading into a registrational trial.",
    summary: "Primary aldosteronism accounts for 5 to 14% of all hypertension and 20 to 25% of treatment-resistant hypertension, and it can be cured by removing one adrenal gland if the excess comes from one side. Establishing which side requires adrenal vein sampling, an invasive procedure that the MATCH investigators describe as requiring a day off work, causing severe pain and hospitalisation in 1 to 3% of patients, available with a success rate above 50% in few hospitals. The consequence they report is stark: fewer than 1% of patients with primary aldosteronism are identified and fully investigated.\n\nThis is not a radiopharmaceutical looking for a use. It is the clearest standalone diagnostic case outside oncology, and it is already being built. 68Ga-PentixaFor from Pentixapharm holds EMA PRIME status granted in October 2024 and FDA Fast Track designation granted in July 2026, with an IND cleared and a phase 3 trial, PANDA, planned at approximately 270 patients in the United States. The academic evidence behind it is substantial: in a 100-patient prospective study, PET/CT agreed with adrenal vein sampling in 90% of patients against 54% for conventional CT, and a meta-analysis of seven studies covering 603 patients has been reported at pooled sensitivity 80% and specificity 91%.\n\nThe agent is also validated against pathology rather than just against another test: consistency between PET uptake and CXCR4 staining was 92.9%, and all 43 CYP11B2-positive functional nodules in one series were CXCR4-positive. What makes this record worth carrying is not that anything is missing, but that it is the field's best worked example of what an unmet-need-first opportunity looks like when it succeeds.",
    asOf: "2026-09-23",
    unmetNeed: "A curable cause of hypertension goes untreated because the test that decides who can be cured is invasive, painful, available in few centres and therefore almost never done: fewer than 1% of patients with primary aldosteronism are fully investigated.",
    useCase: "An endocrinologist or hypertension specialist with a confirmed diagnosis of primary aldosteronism would use the scan to decide between adrenalectomy and lifelong medical therapy, replacing adrenal vein sampling. The value is measured in operations correctly offered, not in lesions detected.",
    hypothesis: "CXCR4-targeted PET will lateralise primary aldosteronism accurately enough to replace adrenal vein sampling, and by removing the invasive step will raise the proportion of patients investigated and cured well above the current figure of fewer than 1%.",
    rationale: "CXCR4 is expressed on aldosterone-producing tissue and tracks the pathology marker: all CYP11B2-positive functional nodules in one series were CXCR4-positive, with 92.9% consistency between PET uptake and staining. Against the invasive reference standard, PET/CT concordance was 90% against CT's 54% in 100 patients, and surgical outcomes support it, with complete biochemical and clinical success of 91.67% and 100% in 36 patients operated on the basis of PET lateralisation. The molecular-imaging approach has already been shown non-inferior to adrenal vein sampling with a different tracer in the MATCH trial, so the regulatory logic is established.",
    test: "The registrational design is already defined: a multicentre trial benchmarking PET lateralisation against a performance standard derived from guideline-based diagnosis, with post-adrenalectomy biochemical and clinical cure as the outcome that matters. The open question this record tracks is whether phase 3 reproduces the 90% concordance seen in single-centre studies.",
